course: politics
lesson: 11
title: Debates and rows on chat shows
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Picture a TV debate as a game of tennis with words: two players, a referee called the moderator, and a clock. Each person gets a turn to answer, then a short turn to reply, called a rebuttal.
explain: When candidates talk over each other, it is like both players serving at once: the fans cheer, but nobody at home can follow the ball.
explain: Debates have no scoreboard. The morning after, each side's helpers tell reporters that their person "won", and people mostly judge by the side they already liked. This telling a story your way is called spin.
explain: Clapping, a funny planned line, a loud guest or a wiggly line on the screen all tell you what some people felt, not whether an answer was true. A careful viewer listens for reasons, checks the claims and notices who is not there.
explain: You will hear the debate talked about in all sorts of voices: a "row" is a loud argument in Ireland and Britain, "the craic" is Irish for the fun or the chat, and a "zinger" is a sharp, funny line planned to make the other person look silly.
fact: A debate has rules agreed before it starts: each person gets a set time, and the moderator asks the questions and keeps the clock.
fact: A studio audience is made of ordinary voters; their cheers and boos are heard at home and can make an answer seem better or worse than it was.
fact: There is no referee or score in a debate, so "winning" just means who seemed better, and each team sends helpers to tell reporters their person won.
fact: A snap poll is a quick survey of viewers right after a debate; some say it is useful, others say it mostly shows who people liked before.
fact: Short, funny lines called zingers are usually written and practised days before, and news shows replay them as soundbites.
fact: Panel shows often invite guests who strongly disagree, because a row keeps people watching; the loudest guest is not always right.
fact: Fact checkers look up the claims made in a debate in official records; "misleading" means partly true but leaving you with the wrong picture.
fact: Leaders do not have to take part in debates; when one declines, a channel may show an empty podium, an "empty chair".
fact: Turning a question towards the topic you want to talk about is called pivoting; talking points are lines prepared in advance.
fact: Bias means leaning towards one side and impartial means taking no side; tougher questions for the person already in charge are often about their record.
fact: The "worm" is a moving line showing the live reactions of a small sample of viewers with dials, not the whole country.
fact: In a real argument you give reasons; a personal attack goes after the person instead of the idea and proves nothing.
fact: People disagree whether moderators should cut microphones when time is up, check facts live, or let the back and forth run.
exercise: Who runs a TV debate, asks the questions and keeps time? | The moderator | The loudest candidate | The studio audience | The camera crew
exercise: What is a rebuttal? | A short turn to answer what the other person just said | A long speech at the very start, before anyone has said anything | A question from the audience | A vote taken after the debate
exercise: What can studio cheers and boos do? | Make an answer seem better or worse than it was | Prove an answer is true | Decide who wins the election | Show what the whole country thinks
exercise: How is the winner of a debate decided? | There is no official score; people judge for themselves | A referee adds up points | The audience votes by law, using buttons on their seats | The moderator announces the winner
exercise: What is spin? | Telling a story in the way that helps your side | Checking a claim against the official records | Switching off a microphone when time is up, so the turns stay fair | Counting votes again
exercise: Where do most zingers come from? | They are written and practised days before | They are made up on the spot every time | The moderator gives them out | The audience shouts them in
exercise: Why do panel shows often invite guests who strongly disagree? | A row keeps people watching | The law says guests must argue | Calm guests are not allowed on TV | Guests are paid to agree with the host
exercise: What does a fact checker do after a debate? | Looks up the claims in official records and trusted sources | Counts how many times people clapped for each of the candidates | Decides which candidate is nicer | Writes new jokes for the next debate
exercise: A claim is marked "misleading". What does that mean? | Partly true, but it leaves you with the wrong picture | Completely made up from start to end, with not one true word in it | Fully true in every way | Too boring to check
exercise: What does an empty podium on a debate show mean? | A leader chose not to take part | The studio ran out of chairs | The debate was cancelled | The leader was not allowed in
exercise: A reporter asks about school lunches and the candidate talks only about jobs. What is this called? | Pivoting | Rebutting | Heckling a rival | Canvassing votes
exercise: What does impartial mean? | Not taking any side | Taking the winning side | Asking only easy questions | Agreeing with the audience
exercise: What does the wiggly "worm" line on a debate show? | The live feelings of a small group of viewers | The votes of the whole country, counted live as people watch at home | Who is telling the truth | How loud the studio is
exercise: In Kai's class debate, what earns points? | Giving reasons | Being the loudest | Calling ideas stupid | Talking the longest
exercise: What is a personal attack? | Going after the person instead of their idea | Giving a reason with a fact | Asking the moderator a question | Answering in the time allowed
exercise: Why might a leader who is ahead decline a debate? | A debate could be too risky for them | Leaders are banned from debates | Debates are only for losers | Debates happen after the vote
test: «she DESTROYED him last night, no cap, she won» says a radio host (no cap = no lie). What is the best reply? | Won what? There is no official score, and fans judge by the side they like | True, a radio host decides who won | True, debates are scored by referees with a points card, just like boxing matches | No, the moderator always wins
test: Dad mutters: "grand debate, the craic was mighty, but sure nobody answered a thing" (craic = fun). What is he noticing? | The show was lively but the questions were not really answered | The debate was boring and quiet, with nobody saying anything at all for an hour | Everyone gave long careful answers | The moderator answered for them
test: A Leeds lad says: "audience were well mardy, booed her proper" (mardy = grumpy). Does the booing show her answer was wrong? | No, it shows what the room felt, not whether the answer was true | Yes, booing proves an answer is false | Yes, the audience checks every fact on their phones before they decide to boo | No, booing means she won
test: «that zinger was bussin', she totally answered the question» (bussin' = really good). What might be wrong? | A funny line can be memorable and still not answer the question | Zingers always contain the full answer, so a good zinger means the question is answered | Zingers are made up on the spot | Funny lines are banned in debates
test: Two guests on a panel show keep shouting about house prices. Your mate says the louder one must be right. What is the best answer? | Loud is not the same as right | The louder one always has more facts | The quiet one must be lying to us | The presenter picks who is right
test: What would happen if a fact check found a claim "misleading"? | It would say the claim leaves people with a wrong picture, even if part is true | The candidate would lose the election at once, because misleading claims are against the law | The claim would be removed from TV forever | The moderator would switch off the cameras
test: In a cartoon, the empty podium wins the debate because nobody argued with it. What is the real point of an empty chair? | It shows a leader said no, and people ask why | Empty podiums get extra points | It means the leader won already | Channels must cancel the debate
test: "Yeah nah, the host was biased, he grilled her harder" says Uncle in Brisbane (yeah nah = no). What else could explain tougher questions? | She is already in charge and has a record to explain | Hosts always pick a favourite on purpose and are paid by the other side to do it | Hard questions mean the host hates her | The audience writes the questions
test: The worm line shoots up when one leader speaks. Your brother says the whole country loves him. What is wrong? | The line shows only a small sample of viewers | The worm counts every vote in the country | The line is always right about the winner | The worm is drawn by the leader's team
test: "Shut up, that's a stupid idea" a boy shouts in the class debate. Why does he get no points? | Calling an idea stupid is not a reason | He spoke too quietly | He agreed with the teacher | He used a rebuttal
test: Bloke on telly: "Mr Moderator, cut her mic, her time's up!" What is the disagreement about? | Whether cutting microphones is fair or too controlling | Whether debates need moderators at all, or should just let the leaders shout | Whether microphones are legal | Whether the audience may speak
exam: Identify the principal function of the moderator in a televised debate between candidates. | To pose the questions and to ensure that each participant adheres to the allotted time | To determine and announce which candidate has prevailed, in accordance with the rules of the broadcaster | To deliver the opening statements on behalf of the candidates | To conduct a vote among the members of the studio audience
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the determination of a "winner" of a televised debate? | No official score exists, and assessments are largely influenced by the prior preferences of viewers | The winner is determined by an independent referee in accordance with fixed rules agreed beforehand by all parties | The winner is the candidate who receives the loudest applause | The winner is declared by the candidate who was absent
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the term "misleading" as employed by fact checkers. | A claim which is partly accurate yet conveys an erroneous overall impression | A claim which is wholly fabricated in every particular, containing no accurate statement whatsoever | A claim which is accurate in every respect | A claim which cannot be checked by any means
exam: Whereas a participant in a debate responds to a question concerning school meals exclusively by discussing employment, identify the term for such conduct. | Pivoting | Rebuttal | Canvassing | Impartiality
